1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,000
Everybody plants a tree.

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Nobody finds out what happened to it.

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:09,000
A birthday, a school drive, a company afternoon.

4
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:12,000
They plant it, they take the photo, they post it,

5
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:14,000
and that is where it ends.

6
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:16,000
The planting is an event.

7
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:19,000
The keeping alive is a year of small boring jobs,

8
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:22,000
and nothing is attached to doing them.

9
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:25,000
We already pay people to do boring things.

10
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:28,000
Pay a credit card bill on time and you earn points.

11
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:32,000
So why is nothing pointed at the thing our lives actually depend

12
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:33,000
on?

13
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:35,000
This is not a timer.

14
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:37,000
Every plant carries its own coordinates,

15
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:41,000
and the schedule moves with the weather actually over it.

16
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:43,000
Rain pushes the next watering out.

17
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Heat pulls it in.

18
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:48,000
A pest check comes round sooner after warm wet days,

19
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:50,000
because that is when pests turn up.

20
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:52,000
Before the camera opens,

21
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:55,000
it tells you exactly what the photograph has to show.

22
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:00,000
The instruction is the check, so following it passes every time.

23
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:04,000
And while the camera is open it reads the frame a few times a

24
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:07,000
second and says the one thing that would make the shot pass.

25
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:08,000
Out loud,

26
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:11,000
because whoever is doing this is holding a watering can.

27
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:14,000
Then it is checked.

28
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:16,000
Each one says the number it measured.

29
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:18,000
Where the photograph came from.

30
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:22,000
The time, taken on our side, never read off the file.

31
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:25,000
The location, against where this plant was registered.

32
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:27,000
And for watering,

33
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:31,000
soil at least five percent darker than that plant's own dry

34
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:31,000
baseline.

35
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:34,000
That number was measured, not guessed.

36
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:36,000
This is the one that matters.

37
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:39,000
If photographing any plant earned points,

38
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:41,000
the points would be worth nothing.

39
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:45,000
So OpenCV finds keypoints in this photograph and in the plant's

40
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:47,000
first one, matches them,

41
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:50,000
and asks whether they agree on a single viewpoint.

42
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:53,000
The same plant returns four hundred and eighty five.

43
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:58,000
A different plant of the same species, in a similar pot,

44
00:01:58,000 --> 00:01:59,000
returns four.

45
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:02,000
It runs on the phone.

46
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:04,000
No photograph is uploaded to do it.

47
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:09,000
Registering a plant checks the photograph before it accepts it.

48
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:11,000
Point it at a notebook and it says so.

49
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:15,000
Point it at a plant and it works out what that plant is,

50
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:17,000
and says how confident it is.

51
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:21,000
Below thirty percent it will not fill the answer in for you.

52
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:22,000
Seventy-eight species,

53
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:25,000
searched by the names people actually use.

54
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:28,000
And five profiles for anything not on the list,

55
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:32,000
because knowing a plant's name and knowing how to keep it alive

56
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:33,000
are different jobs.

57
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:37,000
The whole premise is that people forget.

58
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:39,000
So this cannot wait to be opened.

59
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:43,000
The next few tasks are registered with a clock on our side,

60
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:47,000
and when one comes due the same message goes out on WhatsApp,

61
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:48,000
on email and as a text.

62
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:50,000
A name, a plant and a date.

63
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:52,000
No photographs.

64
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:53,000
No coordinates.

65
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:55,000
Those never leave the phone.

66
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:00,000
Organisations spend heavily on planting drives every year and

67
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:02,000
get back a photograph from planting day.

68
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:06,000
Rooted produces the record of what actually survived,

69
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:08,000
and that record is what funds the rewards.

70
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:10,000
The offers in here are stand-ins,

71
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:14,000
because naming a real company in a demo would put words in their

72
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:15,000
mouth.

73
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:19,000
Two things did not survive contact with a measurement.

74
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:22,000
I tried to work out the species on the device,

75
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:24,000
from colour and texture.

76
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:28,000
It got the right answer first zero times out of seventeen,

77
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:30,000
against eight percent for guessing at random.

78
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:34,000
Neem and curry leaf are both green pinnate leaves,

79
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:36,000
and no histogram separates them.

80
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:40,000
And the check that says this is the same plant used to compare

81
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:41,000
brightness grids.

82
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:44,000
A photograph of a different neem tree walked straight through

83
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:44,000
it.

84
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:48,000
It was the weakest thing in the app wearing the name of the

85
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:49,000
strongest.

86
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:52,000
So it was measured, thrown away, and replaced.

87
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:56,000
Nothing in here reports a number it did not measure.